# Tailwind 4 Conventions

Use Tailwind 4 idioms by default. Prefer stable, explicit, and predictable utilities over legacy Tailwind v3 habits.

## Layout

- For `flex` and `grid` layouts, prefer `gap-*` over `space-x-*` and `space-y-*`
- Keep `space-x-*` / `space-y-*` only when the container is not a normal `flex` or `grid` spacing case
- Prefer `Stack` in `src/app` and `src/features` when the layout matches the project wrapper

## Utilities to Avoid

- Do not use removed opacity utilities like `bg-opacity-*`, `text-opacity-*`, `border-opacity-*`, `divide-opacity-*`, `ring-opacity-*`, `placeholder-opacity-*`
- Use slash opacity syntax instead, for example `bg-black/50` or `text-foreground/70`
- Prefer `grow-*` and `shrink-*` over legacy `flex-grow-*` and `flex-shrink-*`
- Prefer `text-ellipsis` over `overflow-ellipsis`
- Prefer `box-decoration-slice` / `box-decoration-clone` over deprecated `decoration-slice` / `decoration-clone`

## Explicit Visual Defaults

- Do not assume Tailwind v3 defaults for `border`, `divide`, `ring`, `shadow`, `rounded`, `blur`, or `outline`
- Specify `border-*` and `divide-*` colors explicitly when the visual result depends on them
- Specify `ring-*` width and color explicitly when focus styling depends on old v3 defaults
- Use `outline-hidden` when the intent is the old accessibility-friendly behavior that used to be expressed as `outline-none`

## Interaction and State

- Do not rely on `hover:*` as required UX for touch devices; hover should be progressive enhancement
- Prefer explicit state variants and accessible always-visible affordances over hover-only interaction
- Do not depend on `hidden` being overridden by `block`, `flex`, or similar display classes

## Tailwind 4 Authoring

- For custom utilities, prefer `@utility` instead of defining utility-like classes in `@layer utilities`
- Prefer CSS theme variables and modern Tailwind CSS-first configuration over old JS-config habits when possible
- When using arbitrary values with CSS variables, prefer the v4 syntax like `bg-(--brand-color)`
- Be careful with order-sensitive stacked variants because Tailwind 4 applies them left to right
